{"translation": "For, among other things, all the paroxysms in fevers occur either every day, or every third day, or every fourth day: in the majority of diseases, in which there is indeed the greatest need for [481] foreknowledge of the paroxysm that is going to occur, every third day and every day; in a very few, every fourth day. But perhaps one of these more recent sophists says that some periods also occur every fifth day, and every sixth and seventh, and certain others extending to a great many days. One may truly felicitate such people on their wonderful intelligence, if they do not perceive that by what they say they utterly abolish the usefulness of the whole treatise on types. For those who admit that they do not know how to diagnose the kinds of fevers from the first day, and who wait for the days up to the fourth, saying that they have seen neither any quintan period, nor sextan, nor any beyond these, are able, if not on the second and third of the days, then on the three following, to form some expectation about the coming paroxysm. But for those who extend the periods to a great number of days it is impossible to foreknow the paroxysm that will occur. For instance, the paroxysm that occurred on the first day can belong to a quotidian, to a tertian, to a quartan, to a quintan and to all those that follow.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 36, "surface": "προγνώσεως", "english": "foreknowledge", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 48, "surface": "ὀλιγίστοις", "english": "a very few", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 60, "surface": "σοφιστῶν", "english": "sophists", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 67, "surface": "περιόδους", "english": "periods", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 77, "surface": "παμπόλλων", "english": "a great many", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 86, "surface": "συνέσεως,", "english": "intelligence", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 100, "surface": "τύπων", "english": "types", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 184, "surface": "πεμπταίῳ", "eng